WEBVTT

00:00.388 --> 00:04.102
- [Voiceover] "On circle O
below", so this is circle O,

00:04.102 --> 00:06.227
"segment SE is a diameter."

00:06.227 --> 00:09.392
So this is SE, let me color that in.

00:09.392 --> 00:12.757
So this is a diameter, that's
what they're telling us.

00:12.757 --> 00:14.175
And they say, "What is the measure of

00:14.175 --> 00:16.423
"angle ISE?"

00:16.423 --> 00:19.151
So what we care about, the measure of

00:19.151 --> 00:20.521
angle ISE.

00:20.521 --> 00:22.889
I, S, E.

00:22.889 --> 00:25.107
So we're trying to figure out this angle

00:25.107 --> 00:26.163
right over there.

00:26.163 --> 00:27.870
Now like always, I encourage
you to pause the video

00:27.870 --> 00:30.551
and see if you can work
through it yourself.

00:30.551 --> 00:32.629
So there's a bunch of
ways that we can actually

00:32.629 --> 00:34.707
tackle this problem.

00:34.707 --> 00:37.122
The first one that jumps out at me is

00:37.122 --> 00:39.165
there's a bunch of
triangles here, and we can

00:39.165 --> 00:42.787
use the fact that the
angles, interior angles

00:42.787 --> 00:45.283
of a triangle add up to 180 degrees.

00:45.283 --> 00:48.267
So we could look at this
triangle right over here.

00:48.267 --> 00:50.263
And so we know one of the angles already.

00:50.263 --> 00:53.688
We know this angle has a
measurement of 27 degrees.

00:53.688 --> 00:56.207
If we could figure out
this angle right over here,

00:56.207 --> 01:00.526
this would be angle
SIE, then if we know two

01:01.556 --> 01:03.012
interior angles of a triangle, we can

01:03.012 --> 01:04.649
figure out the third.

01:04.649 --> 01:08.178
And this one, SIE, we can figure it out

01:08.178 --> 01:12.300
because it's supplementary
to this 61-degree angle.

01:12.300 --> 01:15.922
So this angle right over
here is just going to be

01:15.922 --> 01:18.058
this angle plus the 61-degree angle

01:18.058 --> 01:20.925
is going to be equal to 180 degrees,

01:20.925 --> 01:23.038
because they are supplementary.

01:23.038 --> 01:25.197
Or we could say that this angle over here

01:25.197 --> 01:27.856
is going to be 180 minus 61.

01:27.856 --> 01:29.342
So what is that going to be?

01:29.342 --> 01:31.164
180 minus 60 would be 120,

01:31.164 --> 01:34.217
and then minus one gives us 119.

01:34.217 --> 01:37.040
119 degrees.

01:37.040 --> 01:40.593
And so this angle that we
are trying to figure out,

01:40.593 --> 01:43.657
this angle plus the 119 degrees, plus

01:43.657 --> 01:45.573
the 27 degrees, is going to be equal to

01:45.573 --> 01:47.233
180 degrees.

01:47.233 --> 01:49.694
Or we could say this angle is going to be

01:49.694 --> 01:53.397
180 minus 119,

01:53.397 --> 01:55.475
minus 27.

01:55.475 --> 01:57.797
Which is going to be equal to,

01:57.797 --> 02:01.489
so let's see, 180 minus 119

02:01.489 --> 02:05.239
is 61.

02:05.239 --> 02:09.116
And then 61 minus 27

02:09.116 --> 02:12.250
is going to be 34.

02:12.250 --> 02:13.307
So there you have it.

02:13.307 --> 02:17.591
The measure of angle ISE is 34 degrees.

02:17.591 --> 02:19.390
Now I mentioned that
there is multiple ways

02:19.390 --> 02:20.353
that we could figure this out.

02:20.353 --> 02:21.793
Let me do it one more way.

02:21.793 --> 02:23.708
So let me unwind everything that I

02:23.708 --> 02:24.428
just wrote.

02:24.428 --> 02:25.496
We already figured out the answer,

02:25.496 --> 02:26.471
but I want to show you that there's

02:26.471 --> 02:30.372
multiple ways that we can tackle this.

02:30.372 --> 02:31.986
So ISE is still the thing that we

02:31.986 --> 02:32.833
want to figure out.

02:32.833 --> 02:34.307
Another way that we could approach it

02:34.307 --> 02:38.034
is we know we have some angles

02:38.034 --> 02:40.274
inscribed angles on this circle,

02:40.274 --> 02:42.585
and we know that if an inscribed angle

02:42.585 --> 02:45.533
intercepts a diameter,
then it's going to be

02:45.533 --> 02:46.306
a right angle.

02:46.306 --> 02:48.267
It's going to be a 90 degree angle.

02:48.267 --> 02:50.868
So this angle right over here is

02:50.868 --> 02:52.499
a 90 degree angle.

02:52.499 --> 02:54.960
And we can use that information to

02:54.960 --> 02:57.038
figure out this angle,

02:57.038 --> 02:58.907
and we can also use that information

02:58.907 --> 03:03.005
if we look at this triangle,

03:03.005 --> 03:06.802
we could use 90 plus 61 plus this angle

03:06.802 --> 03:09.878
is going to be equal to 180 degrees.

03:09.878 --> 03:12.385
So this angle right over here, another way

03:12.385 --> 03:15.915
to think about it, it's
going to be 180 minus 90

03:15.915 --> 03:17.621
minus 61.

03:17.621 --> 03:21.301
Which is equal to, 180 minus 90 is 90,

03:21.301 --> 03:24.459
minus 61 is 29 degrees.

03:24.459 --> 03:27.538
So this one right over here is 29 degrees.

03:29.766 --> 03:32.751
And then we could look
at this larger triangle.

03:35.821 --> 03:38.069
To figure out this entire angle.

03:39.758 --> 03:41.940
If we know this entire
angle, you subtract 29

03:41.940 --> 03:44.738
then you figure out angle ISE.

03:44.738 --> 03:47.861
And so this large, or what I've depicted,

03:47.861 --> 03:51.506
this kind of magenta
measure right over here

03:51.506 --> 03:54.559
of that angle, plus 90 degrees,

03:54.559 --> 03:57.647
plus 27 degrees, is
going to be equal to 180

03:57.647 --> 04:02.024
because they're the interior
angles of triangle SLE.

04:02.024 --> 04:05.518
So this angle right
over here is going to be

04:05.518 --> 04:09.302
180 minus 61

04:09.302 --> 04:11.543
minus 27.

04:11.543 --> 04:13.675
Sorry, not minus 61, minus 90.

04:15.434 --> 04:19.508
It's 180 minus 90, minus 27

04:19.508 --> 04:21.168
is going to give us this
angle right over here

04:21.168 --> 04:23.572
because the three angles add up to 180.

04:23.572 --> 04:27.588
So minus 90, minus 27.

04:27.588 --> 04:31.326
Which is equal to, so 180 minus 90 is 90.

04:31.326 --> 04:34.056
90 minus 27

04:35.953 --> 04:37.753
is 63.

04:37.753 --> 04:39.448
63 degrees.

04:39.448 --> 04:43.569
So this large one over here is 63 degrees,

04:43.569 --> 04:45.926
and then the smaller one is 29 degrees.

04:45.926 --> 04:49.594
And so angle ISE, which
we set out to figure out,

04:49.594 --> 04:53.380
is going to be 63 degrees
minus the 29 degrees.

04:53.380 --> 04:58.163
So 63 minus 29 is once again

04:58.163 --> 05:00.554
equal to 34 degrees.

05:00.554 --> 05:03.027
So the way I did it just
now, a little bit harder.

05:03.027 --> 05:05.023
It really depends what jumps out at you.

05:05.023 --> 05:07.833
The first way I tackled it,
it does seem a little bit

05:07.833 --> 05:09.354
easier, a little bit clearer.

05:09.354 --> 05:10.747
But it's good to see
these different things.

05:10.747 --> 05:13.243
And at least here, we used this idea

05:13.243 --> 05:17.108
of an inscribed angle that
intercepts a diameter.

05:17.108 --> 05:18.664
And if you say, "Hey, how do we know?"

05:18.664 --> 05:20.429
Well, we've proven in other videos,

05:20.429 --> 05:22.577
but it comes straight out of the idea that

05:24.637 --> 05:26.305
the measure of an inscribed angle

05:26.305 --> 05:29.357
is going to be half of
the measure of the arc

05:29.357 --> 05:30.727
that it intercepts.

05:30.727 --> 05:33.525
And notice, it's intercepting an arc

05:33.525 --> 05:36.485
that has a measure of 180 degrees.

05:36.485 --> 05:38.389
It's intercepting an arc that has

05:38.389 --> 05:40.304
a measure of 180 degrees.

05:40.304 --> 05:42.220
And so this angle's
going to be half of that

05:42.220 --> 05:45.203
since it's an inscribed
angle, not a central angle.

05:45.203 --> 05:47.497
So it's going to be a 90 degree angle.
